{
  "dom_id": "step-e7010",
  "name": "Example: Identifying Partitions of Large Sets",
  "problem": "If $X = \\{ 5, 10, 15, \\ldots, 50 \\},$ which of the following collections of subsets of $X$ are partitions of $X?$\n\n- $\\{ \\{ 5, 10 \\}, \\{ 15, 20, 25, \\ldots, 45 \\}, \\{ 50 \\} \\}$\n\n- $\\{ \\{ 5, 10, 15, \\ldots, 30 \\}, \\{ 40, 45, 50 \\} \\}$\n\n- $\\{ \\{ 5, 10, 15, \\ldots, 50 \\} \\}$",
  "worked_solution": "A partition of a set $X$ is a collection of non-empty subsets of $X$ where every element of $X$ is contained in *exactly* one subset. \n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each collection of subsets.\n\n- Collection I is a partition. All the subsets are non-empty, and every element of $X$ is contained in exactly one subset.\n\n- Collection II is *not* a partition because the element $35$ does not appear in any subsets.\n\n- Collection III is a partition. There is only one subset, but that is okay. The subset is non-empty, and every element of $X$ is contained in this subset.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
